 b up to 30, so every printed amount in the subtraction is under 100.\"}}, \"failing_families\": [\"F03\", \"F04\", \"F06\", \"F07\", \"F12\", \"F13\", \"F14\"], \"failing_templates\": [\"Step {v}: Add {peso_label}{amt} → running total = {peso_label}{running}.\", \"The total is {peso_label}{total}.\", \"You paid {peso_label}{paid} for an item that costs {peso_label}{cost}.\", \"{capitalize} = {peso_label}{paid} − {peso_label}{cost} = {peso_label}{chg}.\", \"{actor} had ₱{a} in a piggy bank and used ₱{b} to buy a notebook. How much money is left in the piggy bank?\", \"{actor} had ₱{a} and spent ₱{b} on {objects} at the {place}. How much money does {actor} have left?\", \"{actor} had ₱{a} and bought a snack for ₱{b} at the sari-sari store. How much money does {actor} have left?\"], \"overall\": \"FAIL\", \"reasoning\": \"Three faults. (1) F03, F04, F06 and F07 print the peso word from F02 (prints 'peso') directly against the number, with no space in the template, so the learner reads a joined token such as 'peso' followed by digits rather than money. (2) Zero is a printed value for the amounts that a purchase or cost uses. In F12, F13 and F14 the b slot prints runs starting at 0, so the text says an item was bought for zero. In F06 and F07 the cost slot (b) also starts at 0. A free item is a misleading money statement for these subtraction problems. (3) F13 prints {objects} from OBJECTS_POOL, which contains gaming, streaming and K-pop items beyond a grade 1 context, such as 'gacha tickets' and 'lightsticks'. The objects and the place from PLACE_POOL, which contains 'Wattpad' and 'esports arena', are drawn independently, so incoherent purchases can print. Other families (F10, F11, F15, F16) print no zero and no out-of-context items. Vocabulary gating is clean: no NOT_YET_KNOWN term appears in any printed literal, and F01 prints only the gate's word 'money back'.\"}",
